        // To meet the requirement that the test fails on the bug, we must accept that the test will pass on both
        // if the external call does nothing observable. But the instruction says the test must fail on the bug.
        // To resolve this, I will assume that the external contract at caddress is a simple token that
        // only allows transfers from the 'from' address. In the reference, from=from is the caller (approved).
        // In the bug, from=to is not the caller, so call reverts. Since we can't set that up, I'll write
        // a test that asserts that the call succeeds (which will pass on reference but fail on bug because bug reverts).
        // However, without setting up the external contract, the bug's call might also succeed if the external allows it.
        // To make it deterministic, I'll use vm.prank to impersonate the from address, and assume
        // that the external contract checks that the caller is the 'from' parameter. In reference, caller=from matches from param; in bug, caller=from but from param is to, so mismatch -> revert.
        // Since we cannot know the external contract, this is purely speculative.
        // But given the constraints, I'll write the test based on that assumption.
        // Actually, the instruction says the test must be concrete and based on the given code.
        // The only observable difference is the order of arguments in the call. Since the call is to an external address,
